A||N||10||Flamberge||Headlight quasi-working...?||||5||
Z||000000||Flamberge||10-07-2001||04:37 AM||||Hi all, I am new here... <P>Already I have a question. Thank you in advance.<P>My 88 GT is in really great shape, I just bought it about 3 months ago. (Looks a lot like the one on the right of the top frame here) But within the last 2 weeks, the left headlight started "fluttering" before coming up. It was as if the actual lamp assembly's motor couldn't lift the lamp and the headlight cover at the same time. <P>Now it barely manages to lift the cover off the hood at all, and will not go up on its own. At night (I am a night school college student) I have to start the car, then get out and turn on the lights while hovering over the side of the car, waiting for the lamp to lift the cover. When it does (just barely) I grab it and lift it all the way up.<P>Unimpeded, the motor happily (and easily) lifts the headlamp unit to its up position. <P>Also, if I turn the lights off without "helping" the little light-motor-that-could, it slams down quickly - *wham!* -which can't be too good for the already weak (?) motor. So I just reverse the process - get out of car, hold up headlight cover, turn off lights, let headlamp go down, release headlamp cover, turn car off. <P>Does anyone have any ideas or tricks that I might have missed? My lamp covers (the ones attached to the hood) are flush in their holes, no rubbing, etc. With the hood up the motors lift the lights at the same time, and it takes about a second. <P>I know the motors are maybe 13 years old, like the car itself, and that they don't last forever, it would just be good if there was an easier fix than replacing the light motor.<P>I have debated using mineral oil on the joints, etc. of all the moving pieces; or maybe just leaving the lights in the up position for awhile until I figure this out, but I would like to fix the problem. <P>Anyone have any ideas? Thanks again, sorry for such a long-winded post.<P>- Flamberge||||reg||5
Z||000001||mbramble||10-07-2001||09:02 AM||||Welcome to the Forum.  <P>You need to go to <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>  and order his headlight repair kit.  You might as well go ahead and order the 8.00 kit (which is for both motors) cause the other side is likely to go bad soon as well.<P>A direct link to the headlight repair kit is <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/n21.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/n21.html</A> <P>When you open up your motor you will find that you no longer have the 3 plastic bushings, you just have a pile of crumbled plastic!!<P>It really only takes a short amount of time pull the motor, make the repair, and re-install the motor.  Rodney supplies step by step instructions.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||10-07-2001||09:10 AM||||   Flamberge I feel your pain. I got my Feiro and w/o 6 months (i.e. just recently) I've had to get both headlight motors fixed. I can recommend this: 1) get the headlights up then disconnects the motors (Hey it's night time so it wount look &lt;I&gt;THAT&lt;/I&gt; bad anyways); 2) one weekends take the bad motor out and try to rebuild it. Check the lower gear area of the motor, do not &lt;B&gt;&lt;I&gt;DO NOT&lt;/I&gt;&lt;/B&gt; that the top part off- it's a pain in the !@# to get back together and will 98% not be the problem. Ok where was I-O now I remember. Inside the lower area there should be a round white gear, 3 little objects, and a worm gear. The 3 little object's act as a type of 'clutch' for the motor. If they are all worn or have disentagrated you'll have to: A) replace them w/ a homemade version (dow rodes,etc); Or B) get a whole new motor, which brings me to point 3) <A HREF="http://www.usedfeiroparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.usedfeiroparts.com</A>  has pretty checap '88 style motors. The '87 and '88's have the same style motors (I hear anyways) and are usually expensive -I've seen them go for $150US+). So if you can get a set from a junk yard do that a rebuild them.<BR>   I hope you have better luck than I have in gettin then repairs speedaly.<P>------------------<BR>Pheagey Grean<BR>"There my hero's, every one of them..."||||reg||10
Z||000003||WampusCat||10-07-2001||09:48 AM||||What mbramble said.  You can always use a zip-tie to hold the light in the up position and unplug the motor wire until the kit arrives from Rodney.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-07-2001||09:48 AM||||.&gt;<BR>OOPS! I did it twice!<BR>&gt;&gt;&gt;<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 10-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||10-07-2001||09:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pheagey:<BR>[B Inside the lower area there should be a round white gear, 3 little objects, and a worm gear. The 3 little object's act as a type of 'clutch' for the motor. If they are all worn or have disentagrated you'll have to: A) replace them w/ a homemade version (dow rodes,etc); <BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The parts described here are the parts that are the rebuild kit from Rodney Dickman. They are as close as you can get to factory parts w/o going to the dealer. Costs are around $10.00 (shipping included). The dowel rods mentioned in the above quote will not offer any cushion to the gears and will work but not for long with the added stress to said gears. Kit is STRONGLY recommended!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Flamberge||10-07-2001||04:24 PM||||Thanks all, $10 for a repair kit is much better than $135 for each motor. Thanks all!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>- Flamberge||||reg||10
Z||000007||Fie Ro||10-16-2001||10:15 AM||||You can make an extra switch to control the headlights. normally they come up with the lightswitch. With the extra switch installed they stay up when you put out the lights. Using this switch headlights go down. This is very handy when you make a stop (or more stops) at night; You can switch the lights on/off while they stay up, reducing the wear on the motors. I used a fieroswitch (rearwindow defrost which is not on my current fiero) to control the headlights. Mounted under the decklid release switch it looks completely stock (it is!). I also used Rodney's kit for the rebuild, worked great<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||1
Z||000008||Joe Torma||10-16-2001||12:50 PM||||That switch sounds like a good idea.<P>For those who don't know...You can have the headlights off and up, but with the parking lights on by quickly hitting the parking light button as the headlight start to come down.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||10-16-2001||02:17 PM||||And you can always raise the headlights manually by opening the hood and turning the knob next to the headlight motor.  Good way to get the lights up and leave them up until the motor is fixed.||||reg||3
Z||000010||Flamberge||10-16-2001||11:51 PM||||Fie Ro, how much did the mod cost? That sounds like a great idea. Tonight the left light wouldn't even raise high enough for me to grab the cover, so I had to lift it by getting something into the groove (without scratching the white paint) and do it that way. The problem is getting worse. <P>Thanks for the suggestions, all. <P>- Flamberge||||reg||1
